course: dictionary
lesson: 13814
title: Words that say there is no going back
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how serious something is. Irreparable is one of them. Look at the word in parts: ir- means not, repair means to fix, and -able means can be done. Put them together and irreparable means cannot be fixed.
explain: «The storm caused irreparable damage to the old barn roof.» The word damage tells you something was harmed, and irreparable tells you it cannot be fixed. If you read «The storm caused damage to the barn roof», you know it was harmed. Irreparable adds that the harm is permanent.
explain: Writers use irreparable for serious things, such as damage to nature or a broken friendship. «Their argument left an irreparable crack in their friendship.» Here the word shows the reader there is no easy way back. Test your meaning by swapping in "cannot be put right" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Irreparable means so badly damaged or harmed that it cannot be repaired or put right.
fact: The prefix ir- means not, so irreparable means not able to be repaired.
fact: The adverb irreparably means in a way that cannot be repaired, as in «irreparably damaged».
fact: «Irreparable damage» is often used for nature, such as a forest or a river harmed beyond recovery.
fact: «Irreparable harm» can also mean serious hurt to people, or to a friendship that cannot be mended.
exercise: «The storm caused irreparable damage to the old barn roof.» What does irreparable mean? | Blown away by the wind for a day | Covered in snow | *Too badly damaged to be fixed | Painted in bright colours
exercise: «Their argument left an irreparable crack in their friendship.» What does this suggest? | *There is no easy way to put it right | They fixed it the next morning | It is a small mark that will fade | It was made by a bicycle
exercise: Which part of irreparable means not? | irre | *ir- | repair | -able
test: «The photos were irreparably damaged by the flood.» What does irreparably mean here? | With a loud noise | In a quick and tidy way | *In a way that cannot be put right | In a bright, cheerful way
test: Which would most likely cause irreparable damage? | *Burning down a forest, so the trees and animals are lost for good | A cold morning at school | Losing a pencil in the classroom | Spilling a drink on the carpet and wiping it up
exam: Which word is closest in meaning to irreparable? | Breakable | Beatable | Movable | *Unfixable
